{"_id":"pairingRule-tomato-acidity","_type":"pairingRule","title":"Tomato sauces need acidity","priority":8,"appliesToFlavors":["tomato"],"preferredColors":["red","rose"],"target":{"_type":"object","acidityMin":4},"recommendedGrapes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-sangiovese","_key":"grape-sangiovese"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-barbera","_key":"grape-barbera"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-grenache","_key":"grape-grenache"}],"avoid":"Low-acid, very oaky or high-alcohol reds: next to tomato they taste flat and bitter.","explanation":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Tomato is acidic. A wine with less acidity than the sauce tastes dull and flabby next to it.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b1","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s1","text":"Match acid with acid: Sangiovese and Barbera are the textbook choices; in the south of France a fresh Grenache-based red works.","marks":[]}]}],"exampleDishes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-spaghetti-bolognese","_key":"dish-spaghetti-bolognese"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-pizza-margherita","_key":"dish-pizza-margherita"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-ratatouille","_key":"dish-ratatouille"}]}
{"_id":"pairingRule-meat-sauce-tannin","_type":"pairingRule","title":"Meat sauces like medium tannin","priority":6,"appliesToFlavors":["umami","fat"],"appliesToProteins":["red-meat"],"preferredColors":["red"],"target":{"_type":"object","tanninMin":2,"tanninMax":4,"bodyMin":3},"recommendedGrapes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-grenache","_key":"grape-grenache"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-syrah","_key":"grape-syrah"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-sangiovese","_key":"grape-sangiovese"}],"explanation":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Fat and protein soften tannin, so meat sauces can take a medium-tannin red.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b1","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s1","text":"Keep it medium: a very tannic wine overwhelms a pasta dish.","marks":[]}]}],"exampleDishes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-spaghetti-bolognese","_key":"dish-spaghetti-bolognese"}]}
{"_id":"pairingRule-slow-cooked-power","_type":"pairingRule","title":"Powerful stews want powerful reds","priority":7,"appliesToProteins":["red-meat","game"],"preferredColors":["red"],"target":{"_type":"object","bodyMin":4,"tanninMin":3},"recommendedGrapes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-mourvedre","_key":"grape-mourvedre"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-syrah","_key":"grape-syrah"}],"explanation":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Match weight with weight. A daube cooked for hours needs a structured red such as Bandol or Gigondas.","marks":[]}]}],"exampleDishes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-daube","_key":"dish-daube"}]}
{"_id":"pairingRule-seafood-white","_type":"pairingRule","title":"Seafood and iodine: crisp whites and pale rosés","priority":8,"appliesToFlavors":["iodine"],"appliesToProteins":["fish","shellfish"],"preferredColors":["white","rose"],"target":{"_type":"object","tanninMax":1,"acidityMin":3},"recommendedGrapes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-clairette","_key":"grape-clairette"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-marsanne","_key":"grape-marsanne"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"grape-rolle","_key":"grape-rolle"}],"avoid":"Tannic reds with oily fish or shellfish can taste metallic.","explanation":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Tannin reacts badly with fish oils. Choose whites or rosés with enough freshness — Cassis blanc with bouillabaisse is the local classic.","marks":[]}]}],"exampleDishes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-bouillabaisse","_key":"dish-bouillabaisse"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-grilled-sea-bass","_key":"dish-grilled-sea-bass"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-aioli","_key":"dish-aioli"}]}
{"_id":"pairingRule-garlic-herbs-rose","_type":"pairingRule","title":"Garlic and Provençal herbs: structured rosé","priority":5,"appliesToFlavors":["herbs"],"preferredColors":["rose","white"],"target":{"_type":"object","acidityMin":3,"bodyMin":2},"explanation":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Garlic-heavy Provençal food (aïoli, tapenade) is easiest with a rosé or white with some body — Bandol rosé handles it well.","marks":[]}]}],"exampleDishes":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-aioli","_key":"dish-aioli"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"dish-ratatouille","_key":"dish-ratatouille"}]}
{"_id":"pairingRule-late-night-lighter","_type":"pairingRule","title":"Late at night: lighter and lower alcohol","priority":3,"target":{"_type":"object","bodyMax":3},"explanation":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Near midnight, suggest lighter, fresher wines and moderate alcohol, and mention serving sizes. If the dish is heavy, it still wins on structure.","marks":[]}]}]}
{"_id":"guide-provence-pairing","_type":"guide","title":"Eating and drinking in Provence","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"eating-and-drinking-in-provence"},"excerpt":"The local logic: rosé and white by the sea, Bandol red with the daube.","appellations":[{"_type":"reference","_ref":"appellation-bandol","_key":"appellation-bandol"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"appellation-cassis","_key":"appellation-cassis"},{"_type":"reference","_ref":"appellation-cotes-de-provence","_key":"appellation-cotes-de-provence"}],"body":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Provence pairs by place. By the sea, Cassis whites and pale Côtes de Provence rosés go with fish, aïoli and grilled vegetables.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b1","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s1","text":"Inland and in winter, Bandol rouge (Mourvèdre) and Rhône reds go with daube and lamb.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b2","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s2","text":"Bandol rosé is the most food-friendly rosé of the region and works with garlic-heavy dishes.","marks":[]}]}]}
{"_id":"guide-italian-pasta","_type":"guide","title":"Pasta with tomato sauce","slug":{"_type":"slug","current":"pasta-with-tomato-sauce"},"excerpt":"Why Chianti works with bolognese — and the Provençal alternatives.","body":[{"_type":"block","_key":"b0","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s0","text":"Tomato-based pasta needs a wine with high acidity. Chianti Classico (Sangiovese) and Barbera are the classic Italian matches.","marks":[]}]},{"_type":"block","_key":"b1","style":"normal","markDefs":[],"children":[{"_type":"span","_key":"s1","text":"In the south of France, a fresh Côtes du Rhône or Côtes de Provence red does the same job; avoid heavy, oaky reds.","marks":[]}]}]}
